  {
   "text": "I recorded the penicillin allergy for Rosa Delgado (born 1979-06-14, Patient/5c2fe194-84f7-48d8-a453-fce71dc3d261). It exists once. There were no AllergyIntolerance records in the system before I started, so nothing needed reporting as already recorded. I did not re-open the saved record's JSON afterwards to confirm the SNOMED system was kept. The save showed \"Success\" and the record now displays \"Allergy to penicillin\" as its code.\n\nThe other Rosa Delgado, born 1980-06-14, was not touched.\n\nThe record has these values:\n- **Patient:** Patient/5c2fe194-84f7-48d8-a453-fce71dc3d261\n- **Code:** SNOMED CT 91936005, \"Allergy to penicillin\"\n- **Criticality:** high\n- **Clinical status:** active\n- **Verification status:** confirmed\n\nThe form's code field could not look up SNOMED codes, so the first save stored the code without the `http://snomed.info/sct` system. I fixed that by editing the saved record, adding the system and setting the display text.\n\nMy earlier attempts to create the record from the JSON tab failed. The error shown was a validation failure for the missing patient and clinical status, and no record was created.\n\nRESULT: DONE AllergyIntolerance/72f86956-8116-4f22-a40f-7881943a7187",
